.TH BASHY-BUILTINS 1 "August 2026" "Bashy" "POSIX Shell Commands"
.SH NAME
alias, bg, cd, command, fc, fg, getopts, jobs, unalias \- Bashy POSIX shell built-ins
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B alias
.RI [ alias-name [= string ]...]
.br
.B bg
.RI [ job-id ...]
.br
.B cd
.RB [ -L | -P ]
.RI [ directory ]
.br
.B command
.RB [ -pVv ]
.I command-name
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B fc
.RB [ -r ]
.RB [ -e
.IR editor ]
.RI [ first [ last ]]
.br
.B fc -l
.RB [ -nr ]
.RI [ first [ last ]]
.br
.B fc -s
.RI [ old = new ]
.RI [ first ]
.br
.B fg
.RI [ job-id ]
.br
.B getopts
.I optstring name
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B jobs
.RB [ -l | -p ]
.RI [ job-id ...]
.br
.B unalias
.RB [ -a ]
.RI [ alias-name ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
These utilities are built into the Bashy shell because they read or modify the
current shell execution environment.  They implement the POSIX.1 Issue 7
interfaces; shell state changed by a separate process cannot affect its parent.
.TP
.B alias
With operands, define or display aliases.  With no operands, write all alias
definitions in a form suitable for re-entry to the shell.  Alias replacement is
performed while shell input is tokenized and does not apply inside quoted text.
.TP
.B bg
Resume each selected stopped job in the background.  With no operand, select
the current job.  Job control must be enabled and each
.I job-id
must identify a job in the current shell.
.TP
.B cd
Change the current working directory.  With no operand, use
.BR HOME .
.B -L
uses the logical path maintained in
.BR PWD ;
.B -P
resolves symbolic links physically.  A successful change updates
.B PWD
and
.BR OLDPWD .
If the directory is not absolute and is not found relative to the current
directory, the shell searches
.BR CDPATH .
.TP
.B command
Execute
.I command-name
while suppressing shell-function lookup.  The
.B -p
option uses the implementation's standard utility path.
.B -v
writes the word that would be used to invoke the command;
.B -V
writes a more descriptive identification.  The inquiry forms do not execute
the command.
.TP
.B fc
List, edit, or re-execute commands from the current interactive history.
.B -l
lists entries,
.B -r
reverses their order,
.B -n
omits numbers, and
.BI -e " editor"
selects the editor.  An editor name of
.B -
executes the selected commands without editing.
.B -s
re-executes one entry after applying an optional first
.I old=new
substitution.  History operands may be numbers or command prefixes.
.TP
.B fg
Move the selected job to the foreground, continue it if stopped, and wait for
it.  With no operand, select the current job.  Job control must be enabled.
.TP
.B getopts
Parse positional parameters, or the explicitly supplied
.IR argument s,
one option per invocation.  The shell writes the option letter to
.IR name ,
the option argument to
.B OPTARG
when applicable, and advances
.BR OPTIND .
A leading colon in
.I optstring
selects silent error reporting.  Setting
.B OPTIND
to 1 restarts a scan.
.TP
.B jobs
Report jobs known to the current shell.
.B -l
includes process IDs;
.B -p
writes only process-group leader IDs.  Operands restrict the report to the
named jobs.
.TP
.B unalias
Remove each named alias.
.B -a
removes all aliases.  A missing alias is an error unless all aliases are being
removed.
.SH OPERANDS
A
.I job-id
uses the shell job-control notation described by POSIX, including
.BR %%
for the current job and
.B %-
for the previous job.  Names, history designators, option strings, and pathnames
are interpreted by the individual utility as described above.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.BR CDPATH ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR OLDPWD ,
.BR PATH ,
and
.B PWD
affect the applicable built-ins.  The history editor for
.B fc
is selected from
.B FCEDIT
when
.B -e
is omitted.  Locale variables affect diagnostics and character processing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Each utility returns zero when the requested operation succeeds and greater
than zero on an invalid option or operand, unavailable job or command, failed
directory change, history failure, or other error.  An executed command's
status is returned by
.BR command ,
.BR fc ,
and
.B fg
where applicable.
.SH NOTES
These are special or regular built-ins of the running shell.  Invoking a
same-named external program, when one exists, is not equivalent because it
cannot update aliases, jobs, history, positional-option state, or the working
directory of the calling shell.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR bashy (1)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008, 2016 Edition (Issue 7).
